The memory bandwidth benchmark's kernels run by a GPU process across its
worker teams, on small arrays (benchmarks/bandwidth/bandwidth-process.m): the
arrays' final values and the dot product, which is a loop with a reduction,
are right.
REQUIRES: amdgpu-gpu

RUN: merkc -O2 -fopenmp -fdo-loops -fprocess-declarations --offload-arch=%offload-arch %libomp-flags %S/../../benchmarks/bandwidth/bandwidth-process.m -o %t
RUN: %gpu-env BANDWIDTH_ARRAY_SIZE=100003 BANDWIDTH_NUM_TIMES=5 %t | FileCheck %s

Without -fopenmp the process runs the loops on its own lane.
RUN: merkc -O2 -fdo-loops -fprocess-declarations --offload-arch=%offload-arch %libomp-flags %S/../../benchmarks/bandwidth/bandwidth-process.m -o %t.sequential
RUN: %gpu-env BANDWIDTH_ARRAY_SIZE=100003 BANDWIDTH_NUM_TIMES=5 %t.sequential | FileCheck %s

CHECK: Merkurius memory bandwidth benchmark, GPU process
CHECK: Running kernels 5 times
CHECK-NOT: Validation failed
CHECK: Function    MBytes/sec  Min (sec)   Max         Average
CHECK-NEXT: Copy
CHECK-NEXT: Mul
CHECK-NEXT: Add
CHECK-NEXT: Triad
CHECK-NEXT: Dot
